Existing weaknesses requiring attention

Semantic recall limitation: hybrid.ts currently starts with lexical candidates. rerank.ts only adjusts the ordering or scores of those candidates. Semantic neighbors that have no lexical match cannot normally enter the final candidate set.

Rigid natural-language handling: language.ts and engine.ts include many narrow regular expressions and special cases. This creates brittle behavior when users paraphrase requests, change subjects, use unusual grammar, or combine intentions.

Restricted response composition: compose() largely reformats a single knowledge record into predefined response depths. It does not provide broadly applicable multi-document synthesis.

Limited multi-step reasoning: Diagnostic workflows work through authored graph branches, but Gamma lacks a general-purpose evidence-based planning mechanism.

Limited source utilization: web.ts performs explicitly requested Wikimedia lookup and presents literal extracts, rather than generating a structured, attributed synthesis from retrieved information.

Short-lived contextual understanding: Current context supports bounded references, diagnostic states, and limited conversational memory. It is not a robust representation of goals, facts, corrections, unresolved questions, and completed actions.

Performance regression: The documented initial JavaScript payload grew beyond the original established size budget. Intelligence improvements must not cause uncontrolled bundle growth.

Solve these through structural improvements.

Do not simply add more regexes and records to conceal architectural weaknesses.

