3. IMPLEMENT INTELLIGENT HYBRID KNOWLEDGE RETRIEVAL

This is one of the highest-priority upgrades.

Improve the existing lexical-plus-semantic system into a calibrated, multi-stage retrieval architecture.

Retrieval pipeline

Implement the following conceptual stages:

Stage A — Query understanding

Normalize and decompose the request while retaining its important meaning, technical entities, and exclusions.

Stage B — Lexical retrieval

Use the existing inverted index, BM25, exact matching, and phrase ranking as a foundation.

Stage C — Semantic retrieval

When enhanced understanding has been explicitly enabled and initialized, retrieve top semantic neighbors from the full semantic index, not only lexical candidates.

Stage D — Candidate fusion

Merge lexical and semantic result sets using a defensible, experimentally calibrated fusion method.

Possible techniques include reciprocal rank fusion, weighted scoring, and rank-based methods.

Do not arbitrarily treat uncalibrated similarity as confidence.

Stage E — Compatibility filtering

Apply browser, operating-system, feature, version, negation, and safety constraints.

Stage F — Context-aware reranking

Use supported conversation context and user-provided observations to prioritize relevant records.

Stage G — Acceptance or clarification

Classify results into supported, uncertain, ambiguous, conflicting, or unsupported.

Ask a targeted clarification question when required.

Critical retrieval requirements

Semantic-only candidates must be eligible for consideration when legitimately relevant.

An unrelated semantic neighbor must not bypass safety restrictions or become an accepted answer merely because its embedding similarity is high.

High-risk recommendations need stronger evidence and stricter acceptance criteria.

Exact technical matches should remain reliable.

The system should be especially good at matching realistic paraphrases with little vocabulary overlap.

Example:

“Whenever I leave a lot of pages running, my laptop starts stuttering.”

This should retrieve applicable tab-resource or browser-performance knowledge even without exact authored wording.

Efficiency

* Reuse normalized embeddings.
* Maintain fingerprinted, versioned indexes.
* Avoid rebuilding indexes unnecessarily.
* Use worker-based computation.
* Respect cancellation.
* Limit expensive candidate calculations.
* Support incremental index updates.
* Keep the deterministic path fast.
* Handle missing or unsupported WASM gracefully.

Preserve the existing explicit semantic-model download consent and cache management.

