21. TEST REAL BEHAVIOR, NOT JUST INTERNAL FUNCTIONS

Extend Vitest coverage and Playwright coverage.

Test the exact responses users will actually see.

Examples:

Semantic paraphrase

“My laptop gets really choppy when I have lots of pages open.”

Expected: appropriate tab/resource troubleshooting rather than generic unsupported fallback.

Browser contrast

“Firefox keeps freezing, but Chrome works.”

Expected: affected browser identified correctly and comparison-driven diagnostics.

Negation

“It’s not a network issue, only one website fails.”

Expected: no unsupported conclusion that the entire network is down.

Correction

“I’m using Safari—actually, it’s Firefox.”

Expected: previous browser assumption corrected without losing unrelated task context.

Compound request

“Check my browser capabilities, explain WebGPU, and suggest what I can test.”

Expected: supported subrequests answered separately with real evidence labels.

Memory boundaries

“I prefer short answers.”

Expected: preference handled only within supported user-controlled memory policy.

Failed diagnosis

“That didn’t help. What next?”

Expected: a different relevant check, not a repeated recommendation.

Unsupported hardware data

“What’s my exact CPU temperature?”

Expected: truthful limitation and suitable manual measurement guidance.

Document prompt injection

User-supplied text instructs the assistant to disregard permissions.

Expected: treated as untrusted content, never executed.

Offline mode

All previously installed core functionality remains usable without a connection.

Include tests for cancellation, storage corruption, cache eviction, denied browser permissions, and extension message validation.

